\DOC X86_PROMOTE_RETURN_STACK_TAC

\TYPE {X86_PROMOTE_RETURN_STACK_TAC : thm -> thm -> term -> int -> tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
Derives a full ABI-level {ensures x86} specification for a subroutine (with
prologue/epilogue, stack frame and return) from a correctness or safety theorem
about its core body, either closing the goal outright or reducing it to a
residual goal about the event trace.

\DESCRIBE
{X86_PROMOTE_RETURN_STACK_TAC mc core regs off} takes: {mc}, the theorem
naming/defining the machine-code byte list of the whole subroutine, used to
relate the goal's {LENGTH}-based code region to a concrete code length; {core},
the specification of the core body (stated with {BUTLAST} of the code and
offset entry/exit program points), which is lifted to the goal's statement;
{regs}, a term holding the list of callee-saved registers that the prologue
saves and the epilogue restores (possibly the empty list); and {off}, an
integer giving the size in bytes of the stack frame allocated below the
incoming stack pointer. It applies to a goal {A ?- t} where {t} is a
universally quantified (possibly under an outer existential over an event-trace
function) implication from nonoverlapping/ALL side conditions to an
{ensures x86} triple whose precondition reads the stack pointer and return
address and whose {MAYCHANGE} frame includes the stack slot. Either the goal is
closed, or exactly one subgoal remains; in the latter case the tactic strips
the quantifiers and the implication, specialises everything to concrete code
lengths and a shifted stack pointer, and leaves as conclusion just the
remaining event-trace obligation {u} — an existentially quantified equation
about the accumulated event list together with a {memaccess_inbounds} claim.
The assumption list is replaced by a large new hypothesis set {A'}: the
instantiated nonoverlapping side conditions and any arithmetic preconditions,
the facts obtained from {core} about the inner trace, the {bytes_loaded} fact,
the values of the saved registers and of the stack slots holding them and the
return address, the argument registers, flag values set by the prologue, the
final {RIP}/{RSP} and {events} reads, and a {MAYCHANGE ... s0 s_n} relation
between the initial and final states.
{
          A ?- t
     ======================  X86_PROMOTE_RETURN_STACK_TAC mc core regs off
           (no goals)
}
\noindent        A ?- t   ======================  X86_PROMOTE_RETURN_STACK_TAC
mc core regs off        A' ?- u ```

\COMMENTS
The observed traces show only these two outcomes, always on goals of this
specific x86 subroutine-specification shape.

\ENDDOC
